# How a function is compiled does not hang on how many calls of it the rest of the program holds: a test that calls it twice would run other code than a program that calls it once.
# Only the body of a module is taken to run once: its own text says so. The twin, function-that-the-module-calls-twice-is-built-alike.ir, is this program and one call more, and the check lines on `describe` are the same in both files.
# f1 = describe
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# CHECK: = IsSlotFilled {{*}}key="label"
# CHECK: = GetSlot {{*}}key="label"
# CHECK: = IsSlotFilled {{*}}key="weight"
# CHECK: = PropGet {{*}}key="label"
# CHECK: = GetSlot {{*}}key="weight"
# CHECK: = Add {{*}}form=StringAndPrimitive
# RUN(BYTES): bytes f1 mechanisms=all
# BYTES: Call Stub Concatenate count 2
# BYTES: Call Stub IntegerToString
# RUN(ANSWERS): run-in-shell
# ANSWERS: one:1
